    <br /><br />
    What it shows is what the record says. In 1679 the headmen of the parishes of Ovaro and Luincis in Carnia drew up a
    return of the men who had gone to Istria, and from Mione it names <em>«Zuane De Franceschi con un suo fiolo, Zuane
    d’Erman: questi tre nell’Istria, luogo imperiale»</em> — Zuane De Franceschi with a son of his, and Zuane d’Erman:
    these three in Istria, in the imperial place. Giorgio Ferigo, who printed the return in full, notes that the list is a
    list of <em>weavers</em>. So the burden here is a trade, carried: a loom frame, shears, bolts of coarse cloth on a
    pack mule. They are walking out of the green Carnian valleys, which are behind them, onto the white karst, which is
    ahead — and both landscapes are in the frame because that is the whole of what this journey was.
    <br /><br />
